.TH HMM 1 2026-09-23 "hmm 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hmm \- run commands in workspaces of the working directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hmm
.br
.B hmm run
.RB [ \-\-rm ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR path ]...
.RI [ command
.RI [ argument ...]]
.br
.B hmm ls
.RB [ \-a ]
.br
.B hmm diff
.RB [ \-\-stat ]
.RI [ workspace ]
.br
.B hmm apply
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RI [ workspace ]
.br
.B hmm merge
.RI [ workspace ]
.br
.B hmm rm
.IR workspace ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hmm
runs commands in workspaces: copies of the working directory that a command
may change as it likes, while the working directory and the rest of the
system stay as they are.
It is meant for running coding agents, and anything else you would rather
not trust with your files.
.PP
A workspace is a copy-on-write clone, so making one is quick and takes no
space until the command writes to it, and it includes everything in the
directory: the repository, uncommitted changes, and build output, so builds
start warm.
The command runs in the workspace, which has the same name as the working
directory but lives under
.BR hmm 's
data directory (see
.BR FILES ),
and it is confined there: see
.BR SANDBOX .
.PP
When it makes a workspace,
.B hmm
also records what the directory was, to compare the workspace with later.
If the directory is the top of a git repository, that is the commit it is
at and the tree of its files, uncommitted changes included, which take
little time and no space in the repository; otherwise, it is a second
clone.
.PP
With no command,
.B hmm
lists the workspaces of the working directory, as
.B hmm ls
does.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B hmm run \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIcommand \fR[\fIargument \fR...]]
Make a new workspace of the working directory and run
.I command
in it, or your shell
.RB ( $SHELL ,
or
.B /bin/sh
if it is not set) if no command is given.
Everything from
.I command
on belongs to the command, its own options included, so
.B hmm run claude \-\-resume
passes
.B \-\-resume
to
.BR claude .
When the command exits,
.B hmm
prints where the workspace is, and exits with the command's exit status.
The workspace stays until it is removed.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-rm
Remove the workspace when the command exits, rather than keeping it, as
.B hmm rm
would.
.TP
.BI \-n " name" "\fR, \fP\-\-name " name
Name the workspace
.IR name ,
so that it can be referred to by the name rather than its ID.
A name is letters, digits,
.BR \- ,
.B _
and
.BR . ,
not starting with
.BR \- ,
and is unique among the workspaces of the working directory.
.TP
.BI \-w " path" "\fR, \fP\-\-write " path
Let the command write to
.I path
too, and to everything under it.
Can be repeated.
Commands that keep state in the home directory need it: for example,
.B hmm run \-w ~/.claude claude
lets Claude Code save its sessions and settings.
.RE
.TP
.B hmm ls \fR[\fB\-a\fR]
List the workspaces of the working directory: their IDs, names, whether a
command is still running in them, how long ago they were made, and the
directory they are workspaces of.
With
.BR \-a " (" \-\-all ),
list the workspaces of every directory.
.TP
.B hmm diff \fR[\fB\-\-stat\fR] [\fIworkspace\fR]
Show what changed in
.I workspace
since it was made, or in the working directory's latest workspace if none is
given, as a patch; with
.BR \-\-stat ,
only which files changed, and how much.
If the directory is a git repository, what it ignores, such as build
output, is left out.
.TP
.B hmm apply \fR[\fB\-\-check\fR] [\fIworkspace\fR]
Make the changes that
.B hmm diff
shows in the directory the workspace was made from: all of them or, if any
does not apply, because the directory has changed in the same places since
the workspace was made, none.
Only files change: nothing is staged or committed.
A workspace that is already applied is left alone, and so is one in which a
command is running.
With
.BR \-\-check ,
only say whether the changes apply.
The workspace stays until it is removed.
.TP
.B hmm merge \fR[\fIworkspace\fR]
Merge the commits made in
.IR workspace ,
or in the working directory's latest workspace if none is given, into the
branch the directory it was made from is on, as
.BR "git merge" (1)
does: a fast-forward if the branch has not moved since the workspace was
made, a merge commit if it has, and, if the two changed the same lines,
conflicts to resolve and commit as for any merge.
Only commits are merged: changes the command did not commit are left in the
workspace, and
.B hmm merge
says so.
The workspace must have been made from the top of a git repository, and no
command may be running in it.
The commits stay in the repository as
.BI refs/hmm/ id
until the workspace is removed.
.TP
.B hmm rm \fIworkspace \fR...
Remove the workspaces given, and everything in them, and the references to
their commits that
.B hmm merge
made.
A workspace in which a command is running is not removed.
.PP
.B hmm \-\-help
prints a summary of the commands, and
.B hmm
.I command
.B \-\-help
of a command's options.
.SH WORKSPACES
Every workspace has an ID of six letters and digits, chosen at random when
it is made, such as
.BR k3xq9a ,
and a name if it was given one with
.BR \-n .
Wherever a workspace is expected, it can be given by its name, if it is a
workspace of the working directory, or by its ID, or by the start of its ID
if no other workspace's ID starts the same way:
.B hmm rm k3
removes
.B k3xq9a
if it is the only workspace whose ID starts with
.BR k3 .
Being random, IDs are not handed out again once removed, in practice.
.SH SANDBOX
The command runs under the macOS sandbox
.RB ( sandbox\-exec (1))
on macOS, and under Landlock on Linux, which allow everything but:
.TP
Writing
Only the workspace, the temporary directories
.RB ( /tmp
and
.BR $TMPDIR ),
devices, the caches that builds and package managers write to
.RB ( ~/.cargo ,
.BR ~/.rustup ,
.BR ~/.cache ,
.B ~/.npm
and
.BR ~/Library/Caches ),
and the paths given with
.B \-w
may be written to.
.TP
Reading
The contents of
.BR ~/.ssh ,
.BR ~/.aws ,
.B ~/.gnupg
and
.B ~/.config/gh
cannot be read, and neither can other workspaces.
On Linux, their files can still be listed, and files made in the
directories above them after the command starts cannot be read.
.PP
Everything else, including the network and the GPU, is allowed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/hmm/
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/hmm/
The workspaces, one directory each, named by ID.
In each:
.RS
.TP
.I tree/<name>/
The clone the command runs in and changes, named as the directory it was
made from.
.TP
.IR head ", " fork
If the directory is the top of a git repository: the commit it was at when
the workspace was made, and the tree of its files then.
.TP
.I base/
Otherwise: a clone of the directory as it was when the workspace was made.
.TP
.IR objects/ ", " fork.index ", " base.index ", " tree.index ", " git/
What
.B hmm
keeps to compare the workspace with git: the objects it makes, kept out of
the directory's repository; its indexes of the directory and the workspace;
and, if the directory is not a git repository, a repository of its own.
.TP
.I origin
The path of the directory it was made from.
While a command runs in the workspace,
.B hmm
holds a lock on this file.
.TP
.I name
The workspace's name, if it was given one.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hmm run
exits with the command's exit status, or 128 plus the number of the signal
that ended it.
The other commands exit with 0 if every workspace given was found and dealt
with, and 1 otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run Claude Code in a workspace named
.BR parser :
.PP
.RS
.B hmm run \-n parser \-w ~/.claude claude
.RE
.PP
See the workspaces of the working directory, what one changed, merge the
commits made in it, and remove it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B $ hmm
ID      NAME    STATE    AGE  ORIGIN
k3xq9a  parser  done      2h  ~/src/penberg/dwim
p7mw2d  \-       running   5m  ~/src/penberg/dwim
.B $ hmm diff parser
.B $ hmm merge parser
.B $ hmm rm parser
.fi
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
.B hmm
runs on macOS and Linux.
On macOS, it needs the working directory to be on an APFS volume, the same
one as the home directory.
On Linux, it needs Landlock (Linux 5.19 or later), and making a workspace
takes no space only on a file system that can share blocks between files,
such as Btrfs or XFS, the same one as the home directory; elsewhere, it
copies the directory.
The command cannot run set-user-ID programs such as
.BR sudo (8)
on Linux.
.PP
The command runs in the workspace's path rather than the working
directory's, so tools that remember absolute paths, such as build caches,
may rebuild.
.PP
The network is not confined: a command can send anything it can read to
anywhere, and can push to remotes whose credentials it can reach, such as
through the macOS keychain, or an SSH agent.
.PP
Commits made in a workspace are not signed if signing needs
.B ~/.gnupg
or
.BR ~/.ssh ,
which the command cannot read; the merge commit
.B hmm merge
makes is.
.PP
A workspace of a linked worktree
.RB ( git\-worktree (1))
shares its repository with the worktree, which the command cannot write
to, so commits cannot be made in it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-merge (1),
.BR sandbox\-exec (1),
.BR clonefile (2),
.BR landlock (7)
